Architecture, Power, and Control in Modern Building Types
The sources discuss the relationship between buildings and power by arguing that architectural design is a central mechanism for establishing freedom and control within modern society [1], [2]. This analysis focuses specifically on the critical period of the Enlightenment and the first Industrial Revolution [2].
Centrality of Power and Design

The title of the work, Buildings & Power: Freedom and Control in the Origin of Modern Building Types, implies that power is central to the social process of building [1], [2]. The design, construction, and occupation of buildings are not just matters of aesthetics or technology, but are intrinsically linked to social practice and power dynamics [2], [3], [4].

The exercise of power is formalized and materialized through the architectural design [5]. This involves powerful sponsors (such as owners, inventors, developers, or legislators) choosing resources and imposing social rules that dictate the layout and function of the space [5]. The spatial arrangements themselves embody non-emancipatory social relations [6].

Mechanisms of Control in Building Types
The sources illustrate mechanisms of control across various modern building types:

1. Discipline and Surveillance in Institutions (Prisons, Schools, Workhouses):

• Prisons and Bridewells: Designs emphasize segregation, classification, and isolation to impose a punitive regime and prevent communication, often relying on architectural arrangements to facilitate continuous surveillance [7], [8], [9]. Robert Adam's design for the Edinburgh Bridewell, for instance, used spatial constraint to limit the movements and communication of the confined [10]. Jeremy Bentham's Panopticon is repeatedly cited as the exemplar of this principle, embodying constant visual surveillance and internal, rational power over the soul [8], [9], [11], [12]. The goal of the system was to achieve moral reform by extracting productive labor and preventing prisoner contact [11].

• Schools: The monitorial system, popularized by Bell and Lancaster, utilized buildings as a "vast moral steam engine" to ensure discipline, economy, and moral education [13], [14]. The layout—often using concentric circles, semicircles, or parallel rows—was designed for simultaneous instruction and total surveillance by a master and monitors [15], [16], [17], [18], [19]. This spatial structure served to govern conduct and achieve maximum moral effect at minimum cost [14].

• Workhouses/Poorhouses: These institutions enforced strict classification and segregation of inhabitants by gender and class using separate yards, entrances, and circulation routes [20], [21], [22]. The arrangement of sleeping spaces, for example, often separates 'respectable' paupers from 'unruly' ones [22].

2. Control over Knowledge (Libraries, Museums, Theatres):

• Buildings related to knowledge (libraries, anatomy theaters, museums) functioned as classifying devices to order and control the content or the audience [23], [24].

• Libraries: The spatial organization, such as in Della Santa's ideal public library, was designed to control access to knowledge and ensure efficient circulation [25]. The arrangement of stacks and reading areas often determines who reads what, where, and when [26]. The centrality of the catalogue is symbolic, controlling the physical books and the organization of knowledge [26], [27].

• Lecture Theatres: These spaces, such as anatomy theaters, spatially differentiate the audience and the speaker, organizing the presentation of knowledge in a hierarchical manner [28], [29], [30]. The physical arrangement links the speaker's status to the knowledge being shared [29].

3. Power in Production and Exchange (Factories, Markets):

• Factories and Mills: The spatial layout of industrial buildings, such as cotton mills, transitioned from pre-industrial forms to structures where production determines the spatial and social form [31], [32], [33]. They embody power relations between owners, agents, and workers, where the machine is placed at the center, dictating the labor structure and efficiency [32]. The mill layout, often requiring multiple floors for power shafts and machinery, physically imposed a new system of discipline and interdependence [32], [33].

• Markets and Exchanges: Large commercial buildings are shown to control exchange by creating spatial barriers that separate different categories of goods or traders, often distinguishing the wholesale from retail trade [34], [35], [36]. The London and Amsterdam Exchanges illustrate structures that mediate trade while controlling access and transactions [37], [38].

Freedom and Spatial Constraints

The sources emphasize that the freedom and control dichotomy is manifested through spatial arrangement, where control is often achieved by limiting movement, contact, and visibility [10], [5]. Spaces are defined by rules, leading to the creation of segregated, disciplined bodies or "innocent nature and disciplined bodies" [13]. Buildings thereby become physical arenas where the distribution of resources and power is unequally measured [5]. This control is not necessarily visible everywhere but is often embedded in the functional and spatial features of the architecture [5].

For example, the inverted theater of the Yarrenton's New Harlem design (a school/workhouse complex) reversed the normal dynamic, placing the object of surveillance—the poor—at the stage, and the observer (the auditor/owner) in the domain [39]. Similarly, the spatial layout of hospitals and asylums often enforced moral treatment by segregating the insane from society, using architecture to reinforce internal, rational power over disorder [40], [41].
 
